<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Let <m>\mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Compute <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m> and determine the angle between the vectors.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>Compute the dot product first. Then use <m>\cos\theta = \frac{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}}{\|\mathbf{u}\|\|\mathbf{v}\|}</m>.</p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p><m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v} = -8</m>, and <m>\theta \approx 111.3^\circ</m>.</p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Compute the dot product:
      <m>(2)(1) + (-1)(4) + (3)(-2) = 2 - 4 - 6 = -8</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compute magnitudes:
      <m>\|\mathbf{u}\| = \sqrt{14}</m>, <m>\|\mathbf{v}\| = \sqrt{21}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then <m>\cos\theta = \frac{-8}{\sqrt{14}\sqrt{21}}</m>, so
      <m>\theta = \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{-8}{\sqrt{294}}\right) \approx 111.3^\circ</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Find a unit vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and verify that its dot product with itself is 1.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>Divide the vector by its magnitude.</p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p>
      The unit vector is
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} -\frac{3}{5} \\ \frac{4}{5} \end{bmatrix}</m>,
      and its dot product with itself is 1.
    </p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      <m>\|\mathbf{u}\| = \sqrt{(-3)^2 + 4^2} = 5</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The unit vector is
      <m>\frac{1}{5}\begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} -\frac{3}{5} \\ \frac{4}{5} \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check:
      <m>\left(-\frac{3}{5}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{4}{5}\right)^2 = \frac{9}{25} + \frac{16}{25} = 1</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Determine whether <m>\mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> are orthogonal. If they are not, find the projection of <m>\mathbf{u}</m> onto <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>Compute <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>. If it is zero, the vectors are orthogonal. Otherwise use the projection formula.</p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p>The vectors are orthogonal, so the projection is <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.</p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Compute the dot product:
      <m>(1)(2) + (2)(-1) + (1)(0) = 2 - 2 + 0 = 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since the dot product is zero, the vectors are orthogonal.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, the projection is
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Find a vector in the orthogonal complement of the row space of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>
      A vector in the orthogonal complement must be orthogonal to each row of <m>A</m>. Use dot products to form a system of equations.
    </p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p>
      One such vector is <m>\begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ -3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Being orthogonal to the rows gives
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1 + 2x_2 - x_3 = 0 \\
      x_2 + 3x_3 = 0
      \end{aligned}
      </m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      From the second equation, <m>x_2 = -3x_3</m>. Substituting,
      <m>x_1 - 7x_3 = 0</m>, so <m>x_1 = 7x_3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus
      <m>\mathbf{x} = x_3 \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ -3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
      so choosing <m>x_3 = 1</m> gives the result.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Compute <m>A^T</m> and verify that
      <m>(A\mathbf{x}) \cdot \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{x} \cdot (A^T\mathbf{y})</m>
      for
      <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      and
      <m>\mathbf{y} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute both sides separately: find <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> and dot with <m>\mathbf{y}</m>, then compute <m>A^T\mathbf{y}</m> and dot with <m>\mathbf{x}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>A^T = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
      and both sides evaluate to <m>-2</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      First compute the transpose:
      <m>A^T = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compute
      <m>
      A\mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 0 \\
      2 \amp 1 \\
      -1 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ -4 \end{bmatrix}
      </m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then
      <m>(A\mathbf{x}) \cdot \mathbf{y} = (1)(2) + (1)(0) + (-4)(1) = -2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now compute
      <m>
      A^T \mathbf{y} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
      </m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then
      <m>\mathbf{x} \cdot (A^T \mathbf{y}) = (1)(1) + (-1)(3) = -2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both sides agree, verifying the identity.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let 
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
      1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp -1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      (a) Find a basis for the column space of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      (b) What is the rank of <m>A</m>?
    </p>
    <p>
      (c) Find a basis for the orthogonal complement of the column space of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Row reduce <m>A</m> to identify pivot columns for the column space.
      The orthogonal complement of the column space consists of all vectors
      <m>\mathbf{x}</m> satisfying <m>A^T \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A basis for the column space is 
      <m>
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      The rank of <m>A</m> is 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      A basis for the orthogonal complement is 
      <m>
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The columns of <m>A</m> are
      <m>
      \mathbf{c}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{c}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{c}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{c}_4 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Observe that
      <m>\mathbf{c}_3 = \mathbf{c}_1 + \mathbf{c}_2</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{c}_4 = 2\mathbf{c}_1 - \mathbf{c}_2</m>.
      Therefore, the column space is spanned by
      <m>\mathbf{c}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{c}_2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence a basis for the column space is
      <m>
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since there are two pivot columns, the rank of <m>A</m> is 2.
    </p>

    <p>
      To find the orthogonal complement, we solve
      <m>A^T \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}</m>.
      This is equivalent to requiring <m>\mathbf{x}</m> to be orthogonal to
      <m>\mathbf{c}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{c}_2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let
      <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Then
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1 + x_3 = 0 \\
      2x_1 + x_2 + 3x_3 = 0
      \end{aligned}
      </m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      From the first equation, <m>x_1 = -x_3</m>. Substituting into the second,
      <m>-2x_3 + x_2 + 3x_3 = x_2 + x_3 = 0</m>, so <m>x_2 = -x_3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>
      \mathbf{x} =
      x_3
      \begin{bmatrix}
      -1 \\ -1 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      A basis for the orthogonal complement is
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let 
      <m>
      \mathbf{u}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, 
      \mathbf{u}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, 
      \mathbf{u}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      Show that this is an orthogonal basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>, and write
      <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      as a linear combination of these vectors.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      First verify that all pairwise dot products are zero. Then use
      <m>c_i = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}_i}{\mathbf{u}_i \cdot \mathbf{u}_i}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The vectors are orthogonal, and
      <m>
      \mathbf{v} = 2\mathbf{u}_1 + 1\mathbf{u}_2 + 2\mathbf{u}_3.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Compute dot products:
      <m>\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_2 = 1 - 1 + 0 = 0</m>,
      <m>\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_3 = 0</m>,
      <m>\mathbf{u}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_3 = 0</m>.
      So the vectors are orthogonal.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now compute coefficients:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      c_1 = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}{\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}
      = \frac{3 + 1}{2} = 2,
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      c_2 = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}_2}{\mathbf{u}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_2}
      = \frac{3 - 1}{2} = 1,
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      c_3 = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}_3}{\mathbf{u}_3 \cdot \mathbf{u}_3}
      = 2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>\mathbf{v} = 2\mathbf{u}_1 + \mathbf{u}_2 + 2\mathbf{u}_3</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let 
      <m>
      \mathbf{u}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, 
      \mathbf{u}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      Suppose that these vectors form an orthogonal basis for a subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      (a) Verify that <m>\mathbf{u}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{u}_2</m> are orthogonal.
    </p>
    <p>
      (b) Find the orthogonal projection of 
      <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      onto <m>W</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      (c) Write <m>\mathbf{v}</m> as the sum of a vector in <m>W</m> and a vector orthogonal to <m>W</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the orthogonal projection formula:
      <m>\mathrm{proj}_W \mathbf{v} =
      \frac{\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{u}_1}{\mathbf{u}_1\cdot\mathbf{u}_1}\mathbf{u}_1
      +
      \frac{\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{u}_2}{\mathbf{u}_2\cdot\mathbf{u}_2}\mathbf{u}_2</m>.
      Then subtract from <m>\mathbf{v}</m> to find the orthogonal component.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      (a) The vectors are orthogonal.
    </p>
    <p>
      (b)
      <m>
      \mathrm{proj}_W \mathbf{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      (c)
      <m>
      \mathbf{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      +
      \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a) Compute
      <m>
      \mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_2
      = (1)(1) + (1)(-1) + (0)(0)
      = 0,
      </m>
      so the vectors are orthogonal.
    </p>

    <p>
      (b) Compute
      <m>
      \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 = 3 + 1 + 0 = 4, \quad
      \mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 = 2,
      </m>
      and
      <m>
      \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}_2 = 3 - 1 + 0 = 2, \quad
      \mathbf{u}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_2 = 2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>
      \mathrm{proj}_W \mathbf{v}
      =
      \frac{4}{2}\mathbf{u}_1 + \frac{2}{2}\mathbf{u}_2
      =
      2\mathbf{u}_1 + \mathbf{u}_2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Compute:
      <m>
      2\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      +
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      (c) Then
      <m>
      \mathbf{v} - \mathrm{proj}_W \mathbf{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      This vector is orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf{u}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{u}_2</m>,
      so it lies in <m>W^\perp</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Apply the Gram–Schmidt process to the vectors
      <me>
      \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      to produce an orthogonal basis for the subspace they span.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then normalize your result to produce an orthonormal basis.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Set <m>\mathbf{u}_1 = \mathbf{v}_1</m>. Then compute
      <m>\mathbf{u}_2 = \mathbf{v}_2 - \mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}_1}\mathbf{v}_2</m>
      using
      <m>\mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}_1}\mathbf{v}_2 =
      \frac{\mathbf{v}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}{\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}\mathbf{u}_1</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      An orthogonal basis is
      <me>
      \mathbf{u}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{u}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \\ -\tfrac{1}{2} \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      An orthonormal basis is
      <me>
      \mathbf{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{e}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \\ -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \\ \tfrac{2}{\sqrt{6}} \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Start with
      <m>\mathbf{u}_1 = \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Compute
      <me>
      \mathbf{v}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1, \quad
      \mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 = 2.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      So
      <me>
      \mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}_1} \mathbf{v}_2
      =
      \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \\ \tfrac{1}{2} \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Then
      <me>
      \mathbf{u}_2
      =
      \mathbf{v}_2 - \mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}_1} \mathbf{v}_2
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \\ \tfrac{1}{2} \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \\ -\tfrac{1}{2} \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      This gives an orthogonal basis <m>\{\mathbf{u}_1, \mathbf{u}_2\}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now normalize:
    </p>

    <p>
      <me>
      \|\mathbf{u}_1\| = \sqrt{2}, \quad
      \mathbf{e}_1 =
      \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Also
      <m>
      \|\mathbf{u}_2\| = \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{4} + \tfrac{1}{4} + 1} = \sqrt{\tfrac{3}{2}}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
      <m>
      \mathbf{e}_2 =
      \frac{1}{\sqrt{3/2}}
      \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \\ -\tfrac{1}{2} \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \\ -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \\ \tfrac{2}{\sqrt{6}} \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence <m>\{\mathbf{e}_1, \mathbf{e}_2\}</m> is an orthonormal basis.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let 
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \\
      1 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      Compute the QR factorization of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is, find an orthonormal matrix <m>Q</m> and an upper triangular matrix <m>R</m>
      such that <m>A = QR</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Apply Gram–Schmidt to the columns of <m>A</m>.
      You should find that the projection is especially simple,
      and the coefficients in <m>R</m> come out as integers.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      Q =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
      \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
      0 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      R =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \sqrt{2} \amp \sqrt{2} \\
      0 \amp \sqrt{2}
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let the columns of <m>A</m> be
      <m>
      \mathbf{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Set <m>\mathbf{u}_1 = \mathbf{v}_1</m>.
      Then
      <m>\|\mathbf{u}_1\| = \sqrt{2}</m>, so
      <m>
      \mathbf{e}_1 =
      \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Compute
      <m>
      \mathbf{u}_2 =
      \mathbf{v}_2 -
      \mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}_1}\mathbf{v}_2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      \mathbf{v}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 = 2, \quad
      \mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 = 2,
      </m>
      so
      <m>
      \mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}_1}\mathbf{v}_2
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
      <m>
      \mathbf{u}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Compute
      <m>
      \|\mathbf{u}_2\| = \sqrt{1 + 1 + 1} = \sqrt{3},
      </m>
      so
      <m>
      \mathbf{e}_2 =
      \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
      <m>
      Q =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \\
      \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \\
      0 \amp \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now compute <m>R = Q^T A</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      r_{11} = \|\mathbf{v}_1\| = \sqrt{2}, \quad
      r_{12} = \mathbf{e}_1 \cdot \mathbf{v}_2 = \sqrt{2},
      </m>
      and
      <m>
      r_{22} = \|\mathbf{u}_2\| = \sqrt{3}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore
      <m>
      R =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \sqrt{2} \amp \sqrt{2} \\
      0 \amp \sqrt{3}
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence, <m>A = QR</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let 
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{b} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Find the least squares solution to the system <m>A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then compute the vector <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> and interpret your result.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute <m>A^T A</m> and <m>A^T \mathbf{b}</m>, then solve the normal equations
      <m>A^T A \mathbf{x} = A^T \mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The least squares solution is 
      <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>
      A\mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First compute
      <m>
      A^T =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
      1 \amp 0 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Then
      <m>
      A^T A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 2
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Next compute
      <m>
      A^T \mathbf{b} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1\cdot2 + 1\cdot1 + 0\cdot2 \\
      1\cdot2 + 0\cdot1 + 1\cdot2
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Solve the normal equations:
      <m>
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 2
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      From the first equation: <m>2x_1 + x_2 = 3</m>.
      From the second: <m>x_1 + 2x_2 = 4</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Solving gives <m>x_1 = 1</m>, <m>x_2 = 1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Compute
      <m>
      A\mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      This vector is the projection of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> onto the column space of <m>A</m>.
      It is the closest vector in the column space to <m>\mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A company tracks the number of hours an employee trains and the number of tasks completed during a shift. The data collected are:
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>(1,2), (2,2), (3,4), (4,5)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fit a line <m>y = c + mt</m> to this data using the method of least squares.
    </p>
    <p>
      Interpret the meaning of the slope and intercept in this context.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form a matrix equation <m>A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}</m> where
      <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp t_1 \\ 1 \amp t_2 \\ 1 \amp t_3 \\ 1 \amp t_4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Then compute <m>A^T A</m> and <m>A^T \mathbf{b}</m>, and solve
      <m>A^T A \mathbf{x} = A^T \mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The least squares line is
      <m>y = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{11}{10}t</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The slope indicates that each additional hour of training increases output by about 1.1 tasks,
      and the intercept suggests about one-half of a task is completed with no training (a baseline level).
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We model the relationship as <m>y = c + mt</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
      1 \amp 3 \\
      1 \amp 4
      \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      \mathbf{b} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      2 \\
      4 \\
      5
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      A^T =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      A^T A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp 10 \\
      10 \amp 30
      \end{bmatrix}, \quad
      A^T \mathbf{b} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      13 \\
      38
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Solve
      <m>
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp 10 \\
      10 \amp 30
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} c \\ m \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 13 \\ 38 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Multiply the first equation by 5:
      <m>20c + 50m = 65</m>,
      and the second by 2:
      <m>20c + 60m = 76</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Subtracting gives <m>10m = 11</m>, so <m>m = \frac{11}{10}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Substitute back:
      <m>4c + 10\cdot\frac{11}{10} = 13</m>,
      so <m>4c = 2</m>, and <m>c = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the least squares line is
      <m>y = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{11}{10}t</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This line gives the best linear approximation to the data,
      minimizing the total squared error between observed and predicted values.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

</exercises>

</section>
</handout>